<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Heitersheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1548 CE – 1806 CE · capital Heitersheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The seat of the Knights of Saint John's Grand Priory of Germany, whose grand priors ruled a tiny principality in the Breisgau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Order of Saint John</a>, the Knights Hospitaller, acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heitersheim">Heitersheim</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg</a> in the 13th century, and from 1505 it was the residence of the order's Grand Prior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 1548 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Charles V</a> raised the grand prior to a prince of the empire, making Heitersheim a small principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The grand prior governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heitersheim">Heitersheim</a> and a few neighbouring villages directly and oversaw the order's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which sent knights and revenues to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. The principality lay within the sphere of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/further-austria">Further Austria</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> disputed its full independence. It remained Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heitersheim">Heitersheim</a>, and the Grand Priory was dissolved. The priors' castle, the Malteserschloss, survives as a convent and museum.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-heitersheim.jpg" alt="Principality of Heitersheim" width="320" height="362"><figcaption>Image: Lubiesque · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heitersheim_%26_exclaves_1718.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1548 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Heitersheim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Grand Priory of Germany, Order of St John</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1677&amp;focus=principality-of-heitersheim" aria-label="Principality of Heitersheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4858 501 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Heitersheim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5178 757L5229 694M5178 757L5194 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5194 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5178 757h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5178 757h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Heitersheim. <a href="/atlas?year=1677&amp;focus=principality-of-heitersheim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
